Investment in NLM Photonics, a U.S. Company Developing Organic Electro-Optic Materials for Next-Generation Optical Communications —Strengthening Mitsubishi Chemical Corporation’s position in materials for AI data center optical interconnects—
September 9, 2026
Mitsubishi Chemical Corporation (Head Office: Chiyoda-ku, Tokyo; President: Manabu Chikumoto; hereinafter &ldquo;MCC&rdquo;), through its corporate venture capital arm, Diamond Edge Ventures, Inc. (Head Office: California, USA; President: Curtis Schickner), has invested in NLM Photonics (Headquarters: Washington State, USA; CEO: Brad Booth, hereinafter &ldquo;NLM&rdquo;), a startup that develops organic electro-optic (OEO) materials for next-generation optical communications.
&nbsp;
NLM was founded on more than two decades of OEO research at the University of Washington. Its Selerion&trade; materials are applied to silicon photonic devices, producing hybrid silicon-organic photonic integrated circuits (PICs) that modulate light at higher speed and lower power than conventional inorganic approaches.
&nbsp; 
Hybrid silicon-organic photonic integrated circuits (PICs) 
&nbsp;
The investment was made as part of NLM&rsquo;s Series A2 financing round led by Pangaea Ventures.
&nbsp;
The rapid expansion of generative AI is dramatically increasing the volume of data managed in data centers, placing critical demand on optical interconnects that enable high-speed data transmission with lower power consumption. In this context, the materials used in optical modulation are increasingly recognized as a source of differentiated device performance.
&nbsp;
・Comment by President Curtis Schickner of Diamond Edge Ventures, Inc.:
&ldquo;Optical interconnects have become one of the true bottlenecks in AI infrastructure, and the materials at the heart of the modulator are where much of the remaining performance gain now sits. NLM has taken organic electro-optic materials out of the laboratory and shown that they can be processed in the way the semiconductor industry actually builds devices. That combination of scientific depth and manufacturing realism is why we wanted to be part of this round.&rdquo;
&nbsp;
MCC designates &ldquo;Enable advanced data processing and telecommunications&rdquo; as one of the business focus areas under its management vision, KAITEKI Vision 35. MCC aims to strengthen its understanding of next-generation optical communications, including optoelectronic integration, while creating business opportunities by leveraging its own materials technologies.

How aluminium is made and why it matters
The journey from red rock to the metal we use every day.
Aluminium is everywhere. It&rsquo;s light, strong, and durable, making it the ideal material for things like window frames, vehicle parts, food or drink packaging and power cables .&nbsp; 
But where does aluminium come from and how is it made?
Understanding the journey from red rock to metal helps explain aluminium&rsquo;s versatility and why the way it's produced matters.&nbsp;
Key takeaways:&nbsp; 
Aluminium starts as bauxite, a reddish-brown rock found in the ground. Bauxite is refined into alumina, a fine white powder, which is then smelted into aluminium metal.&nbsp;
Aluminium is widely used because it’s light, strong, corrosion-resistant, conductive, malleable, and recyclable.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Global demand for aluminium is expected to increase by almost 40% by 2030 1 . Producing aluminium is energy-intensive - how it’s made matters.&nbsp;
Rio Tinto is investing in technologies like ELYSIS ® and AP60 while expanding recycling to produce more of our aluminium more efficiently while working to reduce production-related emissions.
How do you make aluminium?
From red rock to white powder &ndash; how we get alumina from bauxite&nbsp;
To make aluminium, we need to start in the ground with bauxite, a naturally-occurring, reddish-brown ore often found in tropical and subtropical regions like Brazil, Guinea and parts of Australia.&nbsp;
Bauxite is known for its reddish hue – a result of the iron oxides present in the ore.
After bauxite is mined, the ore is transported to a refinery where it’s processed to extract a fine white powder called alumina using a multistep industrial process called the Bayer process .
A conveyor &nbsp;at our BC Works operations in Kitimat in Canada moves alumina across the site
From powder to molten metal &ndash; how alumina becomes aluminium&nbsp;
After alumina is refined, it gets transported to a smelter where it's dissolved in a molten bath and an electric current is passed through it, separating aluminium from oxygen.&nbsp;&nbsp;
The result is molten aluminium which is then cast into different forms like ingots, billets or slabs. These can then be rolled, extruded or shaped depending on what customers need.&nbsp;
How is aluminium made?
Before aluminium becomes the shiny metal we all recognise, it starts as bauxite, a reddish-brown ore found in the ground. Bauxite is mined and processed into a fine white powder called alumina which is then used to produce aluminium.
